#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2c90d8-0f2e-5f84-bf67-7620d6f1d308" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Design Matrix When the Regression Curve Contains Zero Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "98ec57bb-04f5-559a-9057-854de19eda7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5612" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](images/fe/fe5d2bbb69e110d08d3231d486af483dc05d995e30907a5f9a62cf46743e81d7.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for a Least-Squares Regression Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f5354fe5-fa4b-5443-a097-40174b0b7b16" :question/math-academy-id "e-5613" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n![](images/17/172f8147d4d58d4d3edc42f96e3fb248f59cb749ed0e387c9c8bcddfec08bf79.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\nX \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/id #uuid "75c0086b-db74-5f01-8d66-f023dd7b575d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6094" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $40$ | $70$ | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](images/93/93a2e368924ac5d892ce203e2b2c2ee0007ebb30bb71476272385d5b26695113.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** | **$\\color{blue}6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}70$ | $\\color{red}120$ | $\\color{red}100$ | $\\color{red}80$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ 70 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 100 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for a Least-Squares Regression Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0367ed7d-e350-54f1-a687-13952716b56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266174} {:answer/id #uuid "1750cd69-1431-5ce9-b1f9-19feb9e4912b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266336} {:answer/id #uuid "60aec3a2-5ceb-5800-ac08-2085df77b7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(X{X}^{T})^{-1}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266998} {:answer/id #uuid "a87229a8-6cb4-5fb4-bec1-22b59f5cfd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267671} {:answer/id #uuid "c76008b7-876a-5ea7-846f-ae0d674ca6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1750cd69-1431-5ce9-b1f9-19feb9e4912b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266336} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cb3711-da1c-58ff-89fa-488b09b80b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c6cfa2-d279-58be-acea-41610b8955d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139593" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\beta =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for a Least-Squares Regression Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a7084c-c30f-5232-858b-9de95f8dc7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} {:answer/id #uuid "7035ae25-bae6-5784-9a22-06a21aa5b21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267131} {:answer/id #uuid "8b24fe95-3529-5193-be67-e03845cb8972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X{X}^{T}\\,(X{X}^{T})^{-1}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267393} {:answer/id #uuid "d598c94a-a6a6-575e-afa4-7a9765d0b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}X{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268047} {:answer/id #uuid "e18490b8-96ff-50e6-aa1e-707f1e27022c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860